      transcriptEN: 'Bank the whole thing. A binary divider splits a dividend by a divisor into a quotient and a remainder, always satisfying Dividend equals Divisor times Quotient plus Remainder, with the remainder less than the divisor. A combinational divider is long division unrolled into a 2D grid. The atom is the controlled subtractor: a full subtractor feeding a 2-to-1 MUX, with the borrow-out as the select, so CellOut equals borrow-prime times D plus borrow times x - keep the difference when it fit, restore the minuend when it did not. Borrow ripples sideways to make each quotient bit, q equals borrow-prime, and the remainder flows down. A standard array uses n rows of about n cells, roughly n squared, to divide a 2n-bit dividend by an n-bit divisor. The same humble full subtractor plus one tiny MUX turns a paper algorithm into a clock-free cloud of gates.',
